2 DATE 13 SEP 1857
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Aadne Nesset and his wife, Ingeborg Ousdal Nesset, lived in the Sirdal Valley of Norway. They had two children. At that time, work in Norway was hard to come by. One might work on the family farm or go into the mer
2 CONC chant marines. Many Norwegians were leaving Norway  Aadne too, chose to make the move. He sailed from Norway to England, then on to New York - a trip of nine weeks. After traveling west, he found work in Tacoma, Washington, at the
2 CONC  time when the railroad was built.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aadne went to live at Saxon. Fourteen years elapsed before Ingeborg saw Aadne again. In 1901, he returned to Norway Then, he told the people "You've got to go to America". In 1902, Aadne, his wife Ingeborg, and three children (Ber
2 CONC tha, age 22; Ole, nearly 21; and Anna in her teens - born after Aadne left for America) made the trip as well as cousins, Lars and Anna Nesset, and their three children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The families made their way to Saxon to the property where the Bakke Farms and Campground is located today. The property was covered with timber. A small clearing provided enough space on which to put a tent on which the family (A
2 CONC adne, Ingeborg, Bertha, Ole, and Anna) lived while the family home was being built. The present large home had three bedrooms at first - one being downstairs. Additions were completed to make a total of five bedrooms. The year was
2 CONC  about 1908 when the parents had sufficient rooms (that included a spare bedroom for company). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Aadne Nesset did not want to farm, he persuaded his son-in-law, Andrew Bakke, and daughter, Bertha, to take over, but allow them to continue to live on the homestead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakkes Farms and Campgrounds - a quiet setting shaded by deciduous and evergreen trees located along the banks of the south fork of the Nooksack River, Watch County. Peaks of mountains - Twin Sisters to the east; Mount Baker yet a
2 CONC  little more to the east! That mountain grandeur could well remind one of Norway! On those premises, both the flag of the United States and that of Norway flew! For about 40 years, the families of Lunden-Gunderson and Bakke have g
2 CONC athered for weekend campouts at this site. Louis Sinnes filed a claim there on 10th of June in 1887 and, with the helps of his brothers (George and Eilef Sinnes), began to develop it. The southern (upstream) portion of the propert
2 CONC y was purchased from the Sinnes family by Aadne Nesset, the other portion, which included the Louis Sinnes home, was purchased by Lars Nesset.
