1 NAME Salve Karlsson /Kvinen/
2 GIVN Salve Karlsson
2 SURN Kvinen
2 _MARNM Fosslid
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 14 AUG 1859
2 PLAC Fosslid
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gnr 15 Ose
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1923
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gully
3 STAE Minnesota
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Salve was the first born child of a family of seven children of Anne Rolvsdatter Ose and Kadel Salveson Kvinne, born in Bygland, Norway on August 14, 1859.  He was baptized on September 11, 1859 and confirmed on Ma
2 CONC y 10, 1874 in the Austad Parish in Bygland.  He lived at Fossli in Ose and as a young man was a farm laborer.  He served in the Norwegian Army - he was a Lieutenant.  In 1892 he emigrated from Norway to America, leaving from Krist
2 CONC iandsand on the Allan Ship Lines.  In June 1892 they landed in New York, then part of the way by train, and finally up the Red River to Fishers Landing near Crookston, Minnesota.  His future wife, Birgit was along with him.  Salve
2 CONC  married Birgit Gundarsdatter Kvaale (born August 2, 1872 at Bygland, Norway) on August 2 1892 in Crookston, Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salve worked at a Lumber Saw Mill in Crookston for a few years.  When the Johnson Township, Polk County, Minnesota area opened for homesteading in 1896, Salve went by foot to the area to stake a claim.  Birgit came later by covere
2 CONC d wagon with their one son Gunder and their one cow.  The mosquitoes were so bad it was almost unbearable.  Besides farming and working the logging camps, Salve was a carpenter and a bridge builder.  Salve died November 12, 1923 a
2 CONC nd Birgit died November 2, 1941.  They are buried at Lund Lutheran Cemetery near Gully, Minnesota.  They were members of the Lund Lutheran Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salve applied for his first papers for citizenship on May 8, 1896, and received his second papers on the 8th of January 1902 which declared him and his wife citizens of the United States.  He received his Homestead Rights to his l
2 CONC and on December 30, 1902.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salve and Birgit had a large family of eleven children with a large number of descendants. 
