1 NAME Ånen (Andrew) Tollefson /Adneram / Sinnes/
2 GIVN Ånen (Andrew) Tollefson
2 SURN Adneram / Sinnes
2 _MARNM Bakke
2 NICK Ånen
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 JAN 1880
2 PLAC Sinnes
2 ADDR
3 STAE Vest-Agder
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 MAR 1965
2 PLAC Acme
2 ADDR
3 STAE WA
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Andrew Bakke (Ånen Sinnes) intended to marry his cousin Bertha Nesset who had emigrated with her family to America. In 1903, Andrew. therefore, made the move, too. When he arrived in America, he found so many Sinne
2 CONC s people that he changed his name to Bakke. Andrew lived in Minnesota and South Dakota for one year, one year in Montana where he herded sheep as he had done in Norway as a young lad, and arrived in Saxon in 1905. (There was one B
2 CONC akke who returned to Norway and kept the name of Bakke.) Andrew married Bertha on June 18, 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Andrew, going to church in Norway was very important. As a boy in Sirdal, he and his siblings would walk several miles to the church. They would leave home on Saturday afternoon and stay with family who lived near the church. I
2 CONC n this way, they would be "on time." When Andrew arrived in Saxon, the people first met in homes for services. Then, he and a few others built a church building across the road from the cemetery. The Free Lutheran Church in Saxon 
2 CONC was built in 1926. The preacher had to be able to preach in both Norwegian and English. The parish was served by Rev. T. J. Moen. Travel was often by horse and buggy or walking. Pastor Moen lived in Bellingham, but he served paris
2 CONC hes in the county as well, preaching in Bellingham in the morning, Saxon in the afternoon, and Lawrence in the evening. The church closed in the early '60s. For a time, American Sunday School Union had services on the premises. Th
2 CONC e property was returned to the original owners. Both church and school have been torn down.
